<section class="reference-section" id="avatars"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Vishnu's avatars</h2><a href="/avatars.html">Return to the index</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The orientation follows the owner-supplied <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition. <strong>1.3.1–28</strong> presents a sequence of manifestations and states that the avatars are innumerable; it is not silently converted into a fixed Dashavatara list. <strong>Bhagavad Gita 4.6–8</strong>, within the owner-supplied Mahabharata collection, supplies the wider language of manifestation and the protection of dharma.</p><p>The Dashavatara index is identified as a widely used devotional arrangement whose ninth position varies between Buddha and Balarama. Completed story links retain their own exact Bhagavata, Ramayana or other source notes. Index-only entries are concise orientation, not new retellings.</p></div></section>
<section class="reference-section" id="mahapuranas"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The eighteen Mahapuranas</h2><a href="/mahapuranas.html">Return to the reference</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The list and defining framework follow the owner-supplied <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press, Volume 2. <strong>12.7.8–20</strong> introduces the characteristics of Purana and explains sarga, visarga, vritti, raksha, manvantara, vamsha, vamshanucharita, samstha, hetu and apashraya. Verse 10 notes that some teachers present five characteristics in a more compact classification. <strong>12.7.22–24</strong> states that there are eighteen and names Brahma, Padma, Vishnu, Shiva, Linga, Garuda, Narada, Bhagavata, Agni, Skanda, Bhavishya, Brahmavaivarta, Markandeya, Vamana, Varaha, Matsya, Kurma and Brahmanda.</p><p><strong>12.13.3–9</strong> gives traditional verse-count claims. The public guide intentionally does not treat those transmitted totals as stable modern-edition page counts. The supplied five-part English Brahmanda Purana, twenty-part English Skanda Purana, two-part Linga Purana, Markandeya Purana and Gita Press Shiva and Bhagavata editions inform the orientation notes for works present in the owner's library. No private scan is distributed.</p><p>Descriptions are concise editorial orientation to broad contents. They do not claim that all recensions have identical chapter order, wording or extent.</p></div></section>
<section class="reference-section" id="philosophy"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Gunas, Purusharthas and philosophical schools</h2><a href="/philosophy.html">Return to the field guide</a></div><div class="reference-content"><h3>The three gunas</h3><p><em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Gita Press, Volume 2, <strong>11.25</strong>, especially 11.25.1–36, distinguishes sattva, rajas and tamas through tendencies in conduct, knowledge, action, dwelling, faith, food and happiness, while directing the listener beyond identification with all three. The site's descriptions are compact synthesis, not translated verses. Compare the supplied Volume 1 at <strong>3.26.10–18</strong> in Kapila's account of prakriti and its transformations.</p><h3>The four purusharthas</h3><p>The same supplied Bhagavata edition names dharma, artha, kama and moksha together in multiple settings. <strong>10.80.45</strong> calls the Veda their source; <strong>11.5.35–36</strong> places them within a devotional argument; <strong>11.21.6–7</strong> discusses disciplined action in relation to the four aims. The page's balancing language is editorial and does not present one life-stage formula as universal.</p><h3>The six darshanas</h3><p>The owner-supplied <em>Nyaya Sutras of Gautama</em> translation and commentary supports the Nyaya orientation: <strong>1.1.1–2</strong> links knowledge of sixteen categories with the highest good, and <strong>1.1.3–7</strong> begins its account of perception, inference, comparison and testimony. The Kapila reader and its supplied Bhagavata references supply the site's theistic Sankhya context; this is explicitly distinguished from every historical Sankhya formulation.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-basics" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Hindu American Foundation · Hinduism Basics</span><small>Owner-approved introductory link · the six schools and the diversity of Hindu thought</small></a><a href="https://www.gitasupersite.iitk.ac.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>IIT Kanpur · Gita Supersite</span><small>Primary text with multiple translations and commentaries</small></a></div><p>The school summaries are deliberately introductory. Vedanta is not reduced to Advaita, Yoga is not presented as exercise alone, and philosophical darshanas are distinguished from Shaiva, Vaishnava, Shakta and Smarta religious traditions.</p></div></section>
